\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}
\usepackage[review]{acl}
% \usepackage{acl}  % uncomment for camera-ready (remove [review])
\usepackage{times,latexsym}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}
\usepackage{graphicx}
\graphicspath{{../../figures/paper/}}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\usepackage{multirow}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage{algorithm}
\usepackage{algorithmic}

\title{Trace Verbosity Degrades SFT Verdict Prediction:\\ A Case Study in Information Density Effects}
\author{Anonymous ACL submission}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
In SFT-based binary verdict prediction for code, complete execution traces degrade accuracy by 18--23 percentage points compared to compact exception events, despite containing strictly more information.
Across four code-specialized models (1.5B--13B, three architecture families), verbose traces plateau near the source-code-only baseline (77--82\%) while exception-only input reaches ${\geq}$99.33\%.
Since exception events structurally encode verdict direction, this ceiling is expected; the non-trivial finding is the degradation mechanism: controlled gradient masking places an upper bound of ${\leq}$4\% on backward-pass gradient dilution under matched input format, assigning the remainder to inference-time signal sparsity (diagnostic tokens constitute ${\sim}$1/500 of full traces).
Format-marker removal and cross-dataset transfer confirm the learned signal reflects diagnostic patterns rather than surface shortcuts.
Under compact input, a 7B SFT model surpasses GPT-4o by 12 points (zero-shot, not directly comparable to SFT), a ranking that reverses without trace information, though this comparison is limited to the ${\leq}$13B code-specialized regime tested.
These results decompose trace verbosity into a two-tier signal hierarchy and quantify why more information hurts for this structurally constrained task.
\end{abstract}

\input{introduction}
\input{related_work}
\input{method}
\input{experiments}
\input{discussion}
\input{conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Scope.}
All experiments use Python-only benchmarks (HumanEval, MBPP-sanitized; 591 problems with simple assert-based tests); generalization to other languages, non-assert test frameworks, and multi-file projects is unverified.
Python's structured exception hierarchy makes exception events particularly informative; languages with less structured error reporting (e.g., C/C++ segmentation faults) may exhibit different signal distributions.
All four models (1.5B--13B) are code-specialized; 30B+ models or general-purpose LLMs may behave differently, and whether larger models can overcome signal dilution in verbose traces is untested.
Training solutions were sampled from Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct; whether the signal hierarchy generalizes to human-written code with different exception distributions is untested.

\paragraph{Variance and robustness.}
Core results are validated with 3 seeds; supplementary comparisons use single-seed runs with Wilson CI estimates for variance.
VOTA's longer sequences are vulnerable to tokenizer-driven truncation: 1 of 12 model-seed combinations suffered catastrophic failure (DeepSeek seed 42: 84.45\%), indicating that VOTA robustness depends on tokenizer efficiency.

\paragraph{Mechanistic analysis.}
The gradient masking experiment isolates backward-pass dilution but does not quantify forward-pass attention effects, leaving a residual confound between gradient and inference-time factors.
CodeLlama \texttt{full\_trace} uses a head-2048 proxy, which may systematically underestimate full-trace performance given that tail-truncation experiments confirm diagnostic information concentrates at the trace terminus.
The VOTA loop compression threshold and assertion-point extraction heuristics were not subjected to sensitivity analysis; different thresholds may shift the Tier~1/Tier~2 boundary.

\bibliography{references}

\appendix
\input{appendix}

\end{document}

\section{Conclusion}

We presented the first systematic component ablation for verdict prediction across four models (1.5B--13B, three families), finding that information density determines learning efficiency: compact exception events converge rapidly while verbose traces oscillate without stable convergence.
Exception events achieve ${\geq}$99.33\% across all four models with consistent cross-model generalization, while lower-density loop signals are model-dependent; generalization beyond LLM-generated Python code remains open.

\section{Discussion}

The central finding is that information density determines learning efficiency for trace-based verdict prediction.
Exception events concentrate the runtime's primary fault signal---error type, stack frame, and message---in a compact format (median 150 tokens), while non-exception components (variable states, loop iterations, control flow) record \emph{how} the program ran, not \emph{whether} it failed.

This density perspective explains three key results: (1)~adding trace information beyond exceptions provides no benefit (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), because low-density content competes for attention; (2)~a tail-truncation budget sweep reveals a sharp phase transition at ${\sim}$500 tokens (Table~\ref{tab:tail_cross_model}), matching the boundary where diagnostic events dominate; (3)~the SFT advantage over regex concentrates on caught-exception boundary cases, confirming semantic signal extraction (\S\ref{sec:ablation}).
The narrow cross-model gap (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}) supports that signal density, not model capacity, is the binding constraint.

\paragraph{Why exception\_only outperforms ae\_only.}
Although \texttt{ae\_only} is a strict superset, it underperforms \texttt{exception\_only} (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}), indicating extra content introduces noise rather than signal.

\paragraph{SFT vs.\ regex: semantic understanding.}
A regex baseline exploits a format marker for near-ceiling accuracy, but a \emph{nomarker} ablation (\S\ref{sec:nomarker}) resolves this concern: removing markers drops accuracy by only ${\sim}$2pp, leaving a ${>}$4pp gap over the best non-trivial regex.
On 8 boundary samples where pass-labeled code triggers exceptions, SFT classifies 6/8 correctly vs.\ 0/8 for regex (McNemar $p{=}.041$), confirming ${\sim}$91\% of the signal is semantic.

\paragraph{Why zero-shot and few-shot prompting fall short.}
Zero-shot prompting spans a 42pp range across models (\S\ref{sec:additional}), which SFT compresses to sub-1pp; few-shot ICL improves marginally, confirming the bottleneck is verdict calibration rather than exception recognition.

\paragraph{Near-perfect floor, not hard ceiling.}
The 2 errors (both \texttt{GeneratorExit}; Appendix~\ref{sec:error_overlap}) are shared across configurations; CodeLlama-13B seed 123 achieves 100\%, confirming 99.83\% is a convergence \emph{floor}, signal-limited rather than capacity-limited.

\paragraph{Tokenizer efficiency.}
CodeLlama's tokenizer produces ${\sim}$20\% more tokens than Qwen's on identical inputs; a label-only loss variant recovers accuracy at tail-1024 but collapses at tail-2048, indicating LoRA long-sequence instability (Appendix~\ref{sec:tokenizer_analysis}).

\paragraph{Tier hierarchy and methodological implications.}
The two-tier structure (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}) suggests a fallback design: extract exception events first, resort to Tier~2 only when no exception is raised.
The near-zero loop benefit on CodeLlama-13B may reflect limited loop-pattern coverage in its pre-training data, combined with a relatively high \texttt{no\_trace} baseline (76.47\%) that leaves minimal headroom for loop-derived signal.
Notably, the Qwen family shows substantial loop benefit at both scales (7B: +10.9pp; 1.5B: +7.2pp), with the larger 7B gain likely reflecting greater capacity to exploit structured loop patterns; both scales extract meaningful signal, confirming loop utilization as an architecture-level property.
The methodological insight---that information density determines learning efficiency---transfers to any trace-based task where compact diagnostic events exist within verbose traces, such as re-analyzing archived CI/CD logs when the original test environment is unavailable.

